why 4 1/4 CROSSING The tenor drops to F under the bass's A for one beat, then returns to B♭. The bass moves B♭–A–B♭ as a neighbor figure, and the tenor gets out of its way.
why 5 1/4 PROGRESSION Over the bass's held G the upper voices move by step, so the chord change on beat 2 is a passing one on the way to V⁷.
why 5 1/2 LEAPS The bass falls B♭–G–C, outlining ii before landing on the root of V⁷. Leaping through chord notes keeps the line clear.
why 16 1/4 DOUBLING The soprano holds D while the tenor passes down F–E–D–C♯, so for an eighth both have D.
why 16 1/2 MELODIC The bass leaps up a major seventh, B♭ to A, instead of falling a step to A. It is that step moved up an octave, which keeps the bass from going too low.
why 17 0 LEAPS The tenor resolves C♯ to D and leaps a fourth to G. The tritone C♯–G spans two chords, so it is not heard as one interval.
why 18 1/2 MELODIC The bass leaps up a seventh, A–G, to put the seventh of A⁷ in the bass. Bach chooses this inversion so the bass can then fall G–F into D minor.
why 18 1/2 LEAPS Alto (B♭–A–E) and tenor (G–F–E–C♯) move into the A⁷ chord at the fermata. Their tritones are spread over several notes and two chords.
why 19 1/4 DOUBLING The bass passes down F–E–D under the soprano's and tenor's held D. The chord over E is a passing one, so no real seventh is doubled.
why 19 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices move down within the D minor chord as the bass completes its descent F–E–D.
why 19 1/2 DIRECT The tune leaps D–A while the bass reaches D by step, both inside the D minor chord. A hidden fifth within one harmony, softened by the bass's step.
why 20 1/4 CROSSING The melody lies low here, so the tenor rises above the alto: tenor F–E–D–E–F over the alto's D–C♯–D. Bach keeps both lines stepwise and lets them cross.
why 20 3/8 CROSSING The tenor's E sits above the alto's C♯, still crossed.
why 20 5/8 CROSSING Still crossed: the tenor's E above the alto's D.
why 21 0 CROSSING The tenor's F is above the alto's D one last time; then it falls to D and A.
why 21 0 DOUBLING This is the cadential ⁶₄ of a cadence in D minor: the D minor chord over A moves to A major at the fermata. Soprano and tenor share F, the chord's third, which then falls to E.
why 21 1/4 DOUBLING The bass passes A–G–A under the ⁶₄; its doubling follows the moving voices.
why 22 0 DIRECT The tune falls F–D while the bass leaps A–D: hidden octaves at a cadence. Bach accepts them here because the tune's line is given.
why 24 1/2 CROSSING Alto and tenor part from a unison C, the alto down to B♭ and the tenor up to D, so for a beat the tenor is on top.
why 25 0 MELODIC The bass leaps up a major seventh, G to F♯, instead of falling a step to F♯. The same note an octave higher, which keeps the bass from going too low.
why 25 1/4 LEADING_TONE The bass walks down F♯–E–D toward the dominant of G minor; its F♯ continues the line instead of rising.
why 26 1/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices step down into V⁷ of G minor, the bass by a fourth; the cadence follows on the next beat.
why 26 1/2 DIRECT The tune falls B♭–G while the bass leaps D–G into the fermata: hidden octaves at a cadence, which the given tune makes hard to avoid.
why 28 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano and alto meet on E in contrary motion, the soprano rising C–D–E and the alto falling G–F–E. The soprano's E rises to F; the alto's keeps falling to D.
why 29 0 SPACING Soprano and alto keep moving apart in contrary motion, F above and D below. The gap lasts a beat and closes as they turn back.
why 29 0 LEADING_TONE The alto's E is part of a falling scale, G–F–E–D, in contrary motion to the soprano. The F it would lead to is in the soprano at the same moment.
why 29 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano and alto both touch E again as they turn around. The music is moving to C major, where E is the third, not a leading tone.
why 32 1/4 LEAPS The bass rises F–B♭ and then leaps an octave within the B♭ chord. The second leap only changes register, so the line stays clear.
why 33 1/4 LEAPS The bass falls B♭–A–G and skips to E for V⁶. Its ends form a tritone, but the line moves mostly by step.
why 36 1/4 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Alto and tenor move from F/B♭ to G/C in parallel fifths on the way into the final cadence. Inner-voice fifths like this are rare in Bach and hard to hear here; in exercises, avoid them.
why 36 1/4 SIX_FOUR A passing ⁶₄ on a weak beat: the C chord over G sits between IV and I as the bass moves B♭–G–F.
why 36 1/4 LEAPS After its leap up to D the tune keeps climbing E–F to the high point, then turns back. The hymn melody is kept as written.
why 37 0 HARMONIC_RHYTHM The F chord arrives on the last beat and carries over the bar line while the bass moves under it. The last phrase lingers on the tonic before the closing cadence.
why 37 1/2 LEAPS The tune drops from its high F and keeps falling G–F to the end. Bach keeps the melody as written.
